    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "All tests pass (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 2/2, 0 failures). The 3 test files are `AcceptanceBase` (abstract), `AuditLogCapture` (helper), and `AcceptanceTest` (the 2 soft-delete tests) \u2014 all accounted for.\n\n## Summary\n\nChanged owner deletion from a hard delete to a soft delete.\n\n**Domain model** \u2014 `Owner.java`: added a `deleted` field (primitive `boolean`, defaults to `false`) mapped to a new `deleted` column, with `isDeleted()`/`setDeleted()`.\n\n**API contract** \u2014 `openapi.yml`: added a read-only `deleted` boolean to the `Owner` schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` exposes it and MapStruct maps it automatically (GET now returns `deleted`).\n\n**Database** \u2014 added `deleted BOOLEAN DEFAULT FALSE NOT NULL` to all four schema variants (h2, hsqldb, mysql, postgres). The seed `data.sql` for hsqldb (used by the test suite) uses positional inserts, so I appended `FALSE` to each owner row; h2/postgres use named-column inserts and pick up the column default.\n\n**Controller** \u2014 `OwnerRestControllerV1`:\n- `deleteOwner` now sets `deleted = true` and saves the owner (retaining the row), still returning `204` (2xx). GET afterwards returns the owner with `deleted: true`.\n- The create endpoint's blocking checks now skip soft-deleted owners: `rejectDuplicateIdentity` (identity check) and `applyHousehold` (household-duplicate check) both filter out `deleted` owners, so a normally-blocking duplicate is allowed when the only match is deleted. I also filtered them out of the soft-match `applyPossibleDuplicate` flag for consistency, since a deleted owner shouldn't be reported as a possible-duplicate target.\n\nA newly created owner has `deleted` false via the Java field default (the create mapper never touches it).",
